WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:07.280
Every cable you install is listed on the
cutsheet and labeled at both ends.

2
00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:10.590
This video shows you how to read both.

3
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:19.471
We will cover device names, panel port codes,
one cutsheet row from A to Z, and labels.

4
00:00:23.621 --> 00:00:29.189
Every device has a name and a location, and
the cutsheet gives both.

5
00:00:30.389 --> 00:00:37.298
A name says what a device is, from big to
small: the site, the pod, the rack in that

6
00:00:37.298 --> 00:00:38.943
pod, and the device.

7
00:00:40.293 --> 00:00:45.499
A location says where it is, like the codes
you learned in lesson two.

8
00:00:46.849 --> 00:00:56.844
Some device names you will meet: tor1 is the
top of rack switch. rmu1 and rmu2 are the two

9
00:00:56.844 --> 00:00:58.177
TROSS units.

10
00:00:58.927 --> 00:01:03.919
And pp1 is the OCS panel on the TPU rack.

11
00:01:08.069 --> 00:01:13.594
A panel port code tells you exactly which hole
on a panel to use.

12
00:01:14.344 --> 00:01:17.843
The guide shows two ways of writing the same
spot.

13
00:01:19.243 --> 00:01:23.915
On the cutsheet you will see codes like r1
dash a1.

14
00:01:24.665 --> 00:01:27.822
That means row one, column a, port one.

15
00:01:29.172 --> 00:01:35.316
On the end of row panel, ports are pre-printed
differently: A1 dash 1.

16
00:01:36.066 --> 00:01:41.122
The letter is the column, then the port, then
after the dash, the row.

17
00:01:42.472 --> 00:01:44.584
Both point to the same hole.

18
00:01:45.334 --> 00:01:49.409
Read each piece by what it is, not by its
position.

19
00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:54.897
And remember, rows count up from the bottom of
the panel.

20
00:01:55.647 --> 00:01:58.485
Row one is always the lowest row.

21
00:02:02.635 --> 00:02:06.880
Every cable you install has one row in the
cutsheet.

22
00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.635
Read it from left to right.

23
00:02:11.035 --> 00:02:14.001
A and Z are the two ends of the cable.

24
00:02:14.751 --> 00:02:18.484
A is where it starts, and Z is where it
finishes.

25
00:02:20.784 --> 00:02:27.803
The A side tells you where the cable starts:
the location, the device name, and the port.

26
00:02:29.153 --> 00:02:33.206
The A panel is the first panel and port it
lands on.

27
00:02:34.556 --> 00:02:40.017
Trunk and leg tell you which big cable is in
the middle, and which leg of it.

28
00:02:41.367 --> 00:02:47.703
The Z panel is the last panel and port, and
the Z side is where the cable ends.

29
00:02:49.053 --> 00:02:55.987
If a row says spare, that leg is landed and
labeled, but not plugged into a device.

30
00:03:00.137 --> 00:03:01.395
Now labels.

31
00:03:02.145 --> 00:03:08.119
Every cable gets the same label at both ends,
and it must match the cutsheet.

32
00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:14.439
Single connectors, breakout legs and copper
runs get a flag label.

33
00:03:15.189 --> 00:03:19.285
It sticks out like a flag, four inches from
the boot.

34
00:03:20.035 --> 00:03:23.853
The boot is the rubber cover behind the
connector.

35
00:03:26.153 --> 00:03:32.818
Trunks get a wrap-around label that circles
the cable, one to two inches from the boot at

36
00:03:32.818 --> 00:03:33.492
each end.

37
00:03:34.842 --> 00:03:37.274
All labels are machine printed.

38
00:03:38.024 --> 00:03:39.539
No handwriting.

39
00:03:40.889 --> 00:03:43.726
Line one is the A device and port.

40
00:03:44.476 --> 00:03:47.612
Line two is the Z device and port.

41
00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:52.759
Trunk labels list all the ports their legs
land on.

42
00:03:53.509 --> 00:03:59.184
One to four in square brackets with a colon
means ports one through four.

43
00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:01.491
Commas mean a list.

44
00:04:02.841 --> 00:04:07.300
And if a label and the cutsheet disagree: stop
and ask.

45
00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:18.708
In lesson four of the course, you will choose
the right label for different cables and spot

46
00:04:18.708 --> 00:04:21.818
label problems in realistic situations.

